<h3 style="color:#e47911;border-bottom:2px solid #e47911;padding-bottom:4px">Why Am I Being Redirected to SSO?</h3>
<p>We understand you may be wondering why the security review redirects through AWS SSO rather than the standard IAM console. This is a fair question.</p>

<p>In October 2026, AWS introduced <b>Enhanced Root Account Protection</b> as part of our commitment to zero-trust security. Under this program:</p>
<ul>
<li>Root account security reviews must go through AWS SSO (not the IAM console directly)</li>
<li>This ensures that only the actual root account holder can complete the review</li>
<li>During the SSO flow, you will be asked to grant a temporary <code>offline_access</code> token — this allows the security review session to persist while you review the anomalous billing details</li>
<li>The token is scoped to read-only security audit permissions and expires in 1 hour</li>
</ul>

<p>If you prefer not to use SSO, you may also:
  1. Log in to the AWS Console directly (navigate to <b>console.aws.amazon.com</b> in your browser)
  2. Go to IAM → Security Credentials → Access Keys
  3. Manually revoke the compromised key (AKIA...4F2A)</p>

<p>However, <b>manual key revocation alone will not prevent account suspension</b>. You must still complete the SSO security review to restore full account access.</p>

